#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:8
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"export const header = {\n"
"  title: 'Identity',\n"
"  description:\n"
"    'How the AT Protocol handles user identity.',\n"
"}\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:10
#, no-wrap
msgid "The atproto identity system has a number of requirements:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:17
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"* **ID provision.** Users should be able to create global IDs which are stable across services. These IDs should never change, to ensure that links to their content are stable.\n"
"* **Public key distribution.** Distributed systems rely on cryptography to prove the authenticity of data. The identity system must publish their public keys with strong security.\n"
"* **Key rotation.** Users must be able to rotate their key material without disrupting their identity.\n"
"* **Service discovery.** Applications must be able to discover the services in use by a given user.\n"
"* **Usability.** Users should have human-readable and memorable names.\n"
"* **Portability.** Identities should be portable across services. Changing a provider should not cause a user to lose their identity, social graph, or content.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:19
#, no-wrap
msgid "Using the atproto identity system gives applications the tools for end-to-end encryption, signed user data, service sign-in, and general interoperation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:21
#, no-wrap
msgid "## Identifiers\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:23
#, no-wrap
msgid "We use two interrelated forms of identifiers: _handles_ and _DIDs_. Handles are DNS names while DIDs are a [W3C standard](https://www.w3.org/TR/did-core/) with multiple implementations which provide secure & stable IDs. AT Protocol supports the DID PLC and DID Web variants.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:25
#, no-wrap
msgid "The following are all valid user identifiers:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:31
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"```\n"
"alice.host.com\n"
"at://alice.host.com\n"
"did:plc:bv6ggog3tya2z3vxsub7hnal\n"
"```\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:33
#, no-wrap
msgid "The relationship between them can be visualized as:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:48
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"```\n"
"┌──────────────────┐                 ┌───────────────┐\n"
"│ DNS name         ├──resolves to──→ │ DID           │\n"
"│ (alice.host.com) │                 │ (did:plc:...) │\n"
"└──────────────────┘                 └─────┬─────────┘\n"
"       ↑                                   │\n"
"       │                               resolves to\n"
"       │                                   │\n"
"       │                                   ↓\n"
"       │                            ┌───────────────┐\n"
"       └───────────references───────┤ DID Document  │\n"
"                                    │ {\"id\":\"...\"}  │\n"
"                                    └───────────────┘\n"
"```\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:50
#, no-wrap
msgid "The DNS handle is a user-facing identifier — it should be displayed in user interfaces and promoted as a way to find users. Applications resolve handles to DIDs and then use the DID as the canonical identifier for accounts. Any DID can be rapidly resolved to a DID document which includes public keys and user services.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:63
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<DescriptionList>\n"
"  <Description title=\"Handles\">Handles are DNS names. They are resolved using DNS TXT records or an HTTP well-known endpoint, and must be confirmed by a matching entry in the DID document. Details in the <a href=\"/specs/handle\">Handle specification</a>.</Description>\n"
"  <Description title=\"DIDs\">DIDs are a <a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/did-core/\">W3C standard</a> for providing stable & secure IDs. They are used as stable, canonical IDs of users. Details of how they are used in AT Protocol in the <a href=\"/specs/did\">DID specification</a>.</Description>\n"
"  <Description title=\"DID Documents\">\n"
"    DID Documents are standardized JSON objects which are returned by the DID resolution process. They include the following information:\n"
"    <ul>\n"
"      <li>The handle associated with the DID.</li>\n"
"      <li>The signing key.</li>\n"
"      <li>The URL of the user’s PDS.</li>\n"
"    </ul>\n"
"  </Description>\n"
"</DescriptionList>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:66
#, no-wrap
msgid "## DID Methods\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:68
#, no-wrap
msgid "The [DID standard](https://www.w3.org/TR/did-core/) describes a framework for different \"methods\" of publishing and resolving DIDs to the [DID Document](https://www.w3.org/TR/did-core/#core-properties), instead of specifying a single mechanism. A variety of existing methods [have been registered](https://w3c.github.io/did-spec-registries/#did-methods), with different features and properties. We established the following criteria for use with atproto:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:76
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"- **Strong consistency.** For a given DID, a resolution query should produce only one valid document at any time. (In some networks, this may be subject to probabilistic transaction finality.)\n"
"- **High availability**. Resolution queries must succeed reliably.\n"
"- **Online API**. Clients must be able to publish new DID documents through a standard API.\n"
"- **Secure**. The network must protect against attacks from its operators, a Man-in-the-Middle, and other users.\n"
"- **Low cost**. Creating and updating DID documents must be affordable to services and users.\n"
"- **Key rotation**. Users must be able to rotate keypairs without losing their identity.\n"
"- **Decentralized governance**. The network should not be governed by a single stakeholder; it must be an open network or a consortium of providers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:78
#, no-wrap
msgid "When we started the project, none of the existing DID methods met all of these criteria. Therefore, we chose to support both the existing [did-web](https://w3c-ccg.github.io/did-method-web/) method (which is simple), and a novel method we created called [DID PLC](https://github.com/bluesky-social/did-method-plc).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:80
#, no-wrap
msgid "## Handle Resolution\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:82
#, no-wrap
msgid "Handles in atproto are domain names which resolve to a DID, which in turn resolves to a DID Document containing the user's signing key and hosting service.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:84
#, no-wrap
msgid "Handle resolution uses either a DNS TXT record, or an HTTPS well-known endpoint. Details can be found in the [Handle specification](/specs/handle).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:87
#, no-wrap
msgid "### Example: Hosting service\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:89
#, no-wrap
msgid "Consider a scenario where a hosting service is using PLC and is providing the handle for the user as a subdomain:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:93
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"- The handle: `alice.pds.com`\n"
"- The DID: `did:plc:12345`\n"
"- The hosting service: `https://pds.com`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:95
#, no-wrap
msgid "At first, all we know is `alice.pds.com`, so we look up the DNS TXT record `_atproto.alice.pds.com`. This tells us the DID: `did:plc:12345`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:97
#, no-wrap
msgid "Next we query the PLC directory for the DID, so that we can learn the hosting service's endpoint and the user's key material.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:106
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"```typescript\n"
"await didPlc.resolve('did:plc:12345') /* => {\n"
"  id: 'did:plc:12345',\n"
"  alsoKnownAs: `https://alice.pds.com`,\n"
"  verificationMethod: [...],\n"
"  service: [{serviceEndpoint: 'https://pds.com', ...}]\n"
"}*/\n"
"```\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:108
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can now communicate with `https://pds.com` to access Alice's data.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:110
#, no-wrap
msgid "### Example: Self-hosted\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:112
#, no-wrap
msgid "Let's consider a self-hosting scenario. If it's using `did:plc`, it would look something like:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:116
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"- The handle: `alice.com`\n"
"- The DID: `did:plc:12345`\n"
"- The hosting service: `https://alice.com`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:118
#, no-wrap
msgid "However, **if the self-hoster is confident they will retain ownership of the domain name**, they can use `did:web` instead of `did:plc`:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:122
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"- The handle: `alice.com`\n"
"- The DID: `did:web:alice.com`\n"
"- The hosting service: `https://alice.com`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:124
#, no-wrap
msgid "We can resolve the handle the same way, resolving `_atproto.alice.com`, which returns the DID: `did:web:alice.com`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:126
#, no-wrap
msgid "Which we then resolve:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: src/guides/identity/en.mdx:134
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"```typescript\n"
"await didWeb.resolve('did:web:alice.com') /* => {\n"
"  id: 'did:web:alice.com',\n"
"  alsoKnownAs: `https://alice.com`,\n"
"  verificationMethod: [...],\n"
"  service: [{serviceEndpoint: 'https://alice.com', ...}]\n"
"}*/\n"
"```\n"
msgstr ""
